Quick read: this is shaping up to be your strongest format proof point yet.

Your Layer 2 doc flagged format (intimate character-in-environment, story-rich, not flat splash art) as "emerging, backed by only 2 pieces." This composition is doing that job better than dragon-guy or chained-guy did. You've got her framed tight between two oversized mushrooms, pocket watches breaking the edges of frame, the "This Way / Nowhere" sign doing quiet narrative work in the midground, the rabbit-butler as a secondary character instead of empty background. That's not incidental set-dressing — it's the same instinct you already showed with the dragon mask charm and the paintbrush prop, just scaled up to an entire environment instead of an accessory. If this finishes well, it becomes a third data point for "format," not just a competition entry.

Where it also lines up with your thematic lane, even inside someone else's IP: the wings are covered in small eye motifs. Whether that's Inkoleia's original design or your addition, it's exactly the kind of "mark that carries weight" language your Layer 1 positioning is built on — a raven/crow-winged figure with watching eyes isn't neutral decoration, it reads as omen or curse. If you have any control over captioning this piece when you post it, one sentence treating those eyes as meaningful (what they see, what they cost her) would make this piece do double duty: competition entry and thematic-lane proof.

Where it's not yet matching your rendering philosophy — and this is the important part:

Your proudest technical signature, in your own words, is no direct lighting on hair — color shifting delicately within a narrow hue range so it reads soft rather than sharp. Right now the hair here is sitting closer to flat-shaded than color-shifted. This is the single highest-leverage thing to fix before you call this finished, because hair rendering is the thing you said people already compliment you on most, and it's currently the weakest-rendered element relative to your own bar.

Second: color temperature. Your bridal piece and cooking piece both work because of a competent warm/cool relationship. This piece is almost entirely cool — violet mushrooms, blue-grey trees, lavender floor. The only warm note is the rose foliage, and it's not touching her. Right now nothing in the scene is casting warm light onto her or the tea set. Even a single warm source — candlelight glow from inside the teapot's steam, a warm rim catching the teacup rims, a faint warm bounce off the tablecloth — would give you the multi-source lighting layering you already understand conceptually, and it would separate her from the background instead of letting her blend into the same cool wash as the mushrooms behind her.

Third: depth. You've talked about foreground blur and layered lighting as deliberate depth tools. The two mushroom caps framing the composition are currently rendered at the same sharpness and saturation as she is — they're competing with her for focus instead of receding to frame her. A foreground blur pass on those (and maybe a slight desaturation/haze push on the castle and forest in back) would let the eye land on her face and the tea-pour gesture, which is the actual story beat of the piece.

If I had to rank the fixes by impact on brand consistency specifically:

Hair — bring back your no-direct-light color shift, this is your signature and it's underbaked right now
Warm/cool split — give her one warm source, don't leave the palette fully cool
Foreground blur on the near mushrooms so the environment reads as depth, not wallpaper
If you write any caption for the post, lean into the eye-marked wings as meaning, not just spooky texture
